#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069979 :tutorial/content "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by trial and error, which involves substituting a number for the variable and checking to see if we get a true statement.\n\nFor example, which of the following values of $y$ satisfy the inequality $y - 1 > 3\\,$?\n\n1. $y=6$\n2. $y=2$\n3. $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 6 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 5 & > 3✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **true** statement, so $y=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 1 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 3 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=3$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I satisfies the inequality." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8259a9f-33bd-5c3f-9005-695f9474fbab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3879 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
